1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,530
Happy Halloween, sweetheart.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:05,056
Look at you in that costume.

3
00:00:05,236 --> 00:00:06,821
A store-bought costume,

4
00:00:06,821 --> 00:00:09,165
with a little card that says flame

5
00:00:09,165 --> 00:00:12,130
resistant, and a pumpkin bucket that lights

6
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:14,888
up, and your mother walking right behind

7
00:00:14,888 --> 00:00:16,404
you with a flashlight.

8
00:00:16,584 --> 00:00:17,838
And that's good.

9
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:19,655
That's how it should be.

10
00:00:19,835 --> 00:00:22,148
But let me tell you how Halloween

11
00:00:22,148 --> 00:00:23,830
was when I was your age,

12
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:25,513
in the nineteen fifties.

13
00:00:25,693 --> 00:00:28,089
Because some of what we did would

14
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:29,686
get us arrested today.

15
00:00:30,037 --> 00:00:32,523
I grew up on Elm Street in

16
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:34,054
Mansfield, Ohio.

17
00:00:34,234 --> 00:00:36,771
And on the thirty-first of October,

18
00:00:36,771 --> 00:00:39,816
sweetheart, the whole town belonged to the

19
00:00:39,816 --> 00:00:40,468
children.

20
00:00:40,648 --> 00:00:41,879
No parents.

21
00:00:42,059 --> 00:00:43,649
No flashlights.

22
00:00:43,829 --> 00:00:45,095
No rules.

23
00:00:45,275 --> 00:00:46,725
So come sit with me,

24
00:00:46,725 --> 00:00:48,827
and I'll tell you everything.

25
00:00:49,007 --> 00:00:51,167
Some of it will make you laugh.

26
00:00:51,347 --> 00:00:52,960
Some of it will make your mother

27
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:53,263
faint.

28
00:00:53,613 --> 00:00:55,076
Before any of that,

29
00:00:55,076 --> 00:00:56,770
there was the pumpkin.

30
00:00:56,950 --> 00:00:59,091
My father bought one at the farm

31
00:00:59,091 --> 00:01:01,499
stand on Route Thirty for a quarter,

32
00:01:01,499 --> 00:01:03,171
and on the kitchen table,

33
00:01:03,171 --> 00:01:05,178
on top of the Mansfield paper,

34
00:01:05,178 --> 00:01:07,318
he handed Danny a carving knife.

35
00:01:07,498 --> 00:01:09,109
A real kitchen knife,

36
00:01:09,109 --> 00:01:10,030
the big one,

37
00:01:10,030 --> 00:01:11,411
to a six-year-old.

38
00:01:11,591 --> 00:01:12,983
Danny cut the eyes,

39
00:01:12,983 --> 00:01:14,815
and Father cut the mouth,

40
00:01:14,815 --> 00:01:16,648
and nobody lost a finger,

41
00:01:16,648 --> 00:01:19,799
which my mother called a Halloween miracle.

42
00:01:20,149 --> 00:01:22,938
Then we scooped out the insides with

43
00:01:22,938 --> 00:01:24,101
our bare hands,

44
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:25,573
all slimy and cold,

45
00:01:25,573 --> 00:01:28,208
and my mother roasted the seeds in

46
00:01:28,208 --> 00:01:29,680
the oven with salt.

47
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:32,705
Nothing went to waste in our house.

48
00:01:32,885 --> 00:01:35,381
Not even the guts of a pumpkin.

49
00:01:35,561 --> 00:01:36,683
And that night,

50
00:01:36,683 --> 00:01:39,003
we put a real candle inside it,

51
00:01:39,003 --> 00:01:41,099
lit it with a kitchen match,

52
00:01:41,099 --> 00:01:43,269
and set it on the porch right

53
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:44,541
next to the door,

54
00:01:44,541 --> 00:01:47,235
where every child in a paper costume

55
00:01:47,235 --> 00:01:48,657
would walk past it.

56
00:01:49,007 --> 00:01:50,109
Number one.

57
00:01:50,289 --> 00:01:52,008
Nobody's parents came.

58
00:01:52,188 --> 00:01:53,163
Not one.

59
00:01:53,343 --> 00:01:55,841
My mother would tie my mask on,

60
00:01:55,841 --> 00:01:57,532
give me a pillowcase,

61
00:01:57,532 --> 00:01:59,547
and say, be home by nine,

62
00:01:59,547 --> 00:02:01,319
and take your brother.

63
00:02:01,499 --> 00:02:03,136
I was nine years old.

64
00:02:03,316 --> 00:02:04,953
Danny was six.

65
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:06,410
And off we went,

66
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:07,527
into the dark,

67
00:02:07,527 --> 00:02:08,804
for three hours,

68
00:02:08,804 --> 00:02:09,921
all over town,

69
00:02:09,921 --> 00:02:13,113
knocking on the doors of total strangers

70
00:02:13,113 --> 00:02:15,187
and asking them for candy.

71
00:02:15,538 --> 00:02:17,163
We went in packs.

72
00:02:17,343 --> 00:02:19,826
Ten, twelve kids from our street,

73
00:02:19,826 --> 00:02:22,008
the big ones in front and the

74
00:02:22,008 --> 00:02:25,168
little ones holding on to somebody's coat.

75
00:02:25,348 --> 00:02:27,340
We went to houses three or four

76
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:29,589
streets away where we didn't know a

77
00:02:29,589 --> 00:02:29,911
soul.

78
00:02:30,091 --> 00:02:32,559
And if a man answered the door

79
00:02:32,559 --> 00:02:34,041
in his undershirt,

80
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:35,275
holding a beer,

81
00:02:35,275 --> 00:02:37,826
we said trick or treat and held

82
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:39,472
out our pillowcases.

83
00:02:39,652 --> 00:02:41,440
Nobody thought twice about it.

84
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:42,881
Number two.

85
00:02:43,061 --> 00:02:44,373
The costumes.

86
00:02:44,553 --> 00:02:46,712
Most of ours were homemade.

87
00:02:46,892 --> 00:02:49,280
A sheet with two holes cut in

88
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:50,515
it was a ghost.

89
00:02:50,695 --> 00:02:53,438
Your father's old jacket and a burnt

90
00:02:53,438 --> 00:02:55,724
cork rubbed on your face was a

91
00:02:55,724 --> 00:02:56,105
hobo.

92
00:02:56,285 --> 00:02:58,617
Nobody ever told us that was a

93
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:00,871
strange thing to dress up as.

94
00:03:01,051 --> 00:03:02,996
And a lot of children went as

95
00:03:02,996 --> 00:03:05,881
things nobody would dream of wearing today.

96
00:03:06,231 --> 00:03:08,661
The store-bought ones came in a box

97
00:03:08,661 --> 00:03:10,884
from Ben Cooper or Collegeville,

98
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:13,175
for about two dollars at the five

99
00:03:13,175 --> 00:03:13,800
and dime.

100
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:15,978
A plastic mask held on with a

101
00:03:15,978 --> 00:03:18,526
thin elastic band that snapped in the

102
00:03:18,526 --> 00:03:19,904
middle of the night,

103
00:03:19,904 --> 00:03:22,384
and a costume made of something like

104
00:03:22,384 --> 00:03:23,210
shiny paper.

105
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:26,810
And sweetheart, some of those costumes caught

106
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,014
fire if you so much as walked

107
00:03:29,014 --> 00:03:30,078
past a candle.

108
00:03:30,428 --> 00:03:31,798
That's not a joke.

109
00:03:31,978 --> 00:03:33,585
Children were burned,

110
00:03:33,585 --> 00:03:37,106
badly, wearing rayon costumes and cowboy chaps

111
00:03:37,106 --> 00:03:40,244
near the jack-o'-lanterns on the porches.

112
00:03:40,424 --> 00:03:43,310
Real candles, inside the pumpkins,

113
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:44,499
on every step.

114
00:03:44,679 --> 00:03:46,453
In nineteen fifty-three,

115
00:03:46,453 --> 00:03:49,558
Congress passed a law called the Flammable

116
00:03:49,558 --> 00:03:52,884
Fabrics Act, because of sweaters and costumes

117
00:03:52,884 --> 00:03:54,954
that went up like newspaper.

118
00:03:55,134 --> 00:03:57,560
That little card on your costume?

119
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,493
That's why it's there.

120
00:03:59,844 --> 00:04:01,167
And the masks.

121
00:04:01,347 --> 00:04:03,843
You could barely see out of them.

122
00:04:04,023 --> 00:04:05,346
Two tiny eyeholes,

123
00:04:05,346 --> 00:04:07,917
and your breath made the inside wet

124
00:04:07,917 --> 00:04:08,505
and hot.

125
00:04:08,685 --> 00:04:10,995
We'd run across the street in the

126
00:04:10,995 --> 00:04:12,744
dark with those masks on,

127
00:04:12,744 --> 00:04:13,934
with cars coming,

128
00:04:13,934 --> 00:04:15,964
and somehow most of us lived.

129
00:04:16,144 --> 00:04:18,694
My friend Linda ran straight into a

130
00:04:18,694 --> 00:04:21,682
mailbox in nineteen fifty-three and had a

131
00:04:21,682 --> 00:04:23,795
black eye until Thanksgiving.

132
00:04:24,145 --> 00:04:25,457
Number three.

133
00:04:25,637 --> 00:04:26,658
The candy.

134
00:04:26,838 --> 00:04:29,439
Nobody checked a single piece.

135
00:04:29,619 --> 00:04:31,636
And half of what we got wasn't

136
00:04:31,636 --> 00:04:32,510
even wrapped.

137
00:04:32,690 --> 00:04:33,769
Mrs.

138
00:04:33,949 --> 00:04:37,024
Dietrich on the corner made popcorn balls

139
00:04:37,024 --> 00:04:39,873
with molasses, and wrapped them in wax

140
00:04:39,873 --> 00:04:42,123
paper with a twist at the top.

141
00:04:42,303 --> 00:04:43,197
Mr.

142
00:04:43,377 --> 00:04:44,712
and Mrs.

143
00:04:44,892 --> 00:04:47,829
Hale gave out caramel apples on sticks.

144
00:04:48,009 --> 00:04:51,568
Somebody always gave you homemade fudge,

145
00:04:51,568 --> 00:04:54,592
loose, right into your pillowcase.

146
00:04:54,942 --> 00:04:57,449
Other houses gave out pennies.

147
00:04:57,629 --> 00:04:58,656
A whole penny,

148
00:04:58,656 --> 00:05:00,857
or a nickel if they were rich.

149
00:05:01,037 --> 00:05:03,491
One lady on Park Avenue dropped a

150
00:05:03,491 --> 00:05:05,796
hot penny into your hand from a

151
00:05:05,796 --> 00:05:07,432
frying pan, as a joke,

152
00:05:07,432 --> 00:05:10,034
and laughed while you hopped up and

153
00:05:10,034 --> 00:05:10,406
down.

154
00:05:10,586 --> 00:05:12,691
And the grumpy old man on Third

155
00:05:12,691 --> 00:05:14,253
Street gave out apples,

156
00:05:14,253 --> 00:05:16,290
and the boys threw them at his

157
00:05:16,290 --> 00:05:17,784
garage on the way out.

158
00:05:18,135 --> 00:05:19,690
Then the store candy.

159
00:05:19,870 --> 00:05:22,448
Candy cigarettes, sweetheart.

160
00:05:22,628 --> 00:05:25,252
Little white sticks with red tips,

161
00:05:25,252 --> 00:05:27,567
in a box that looked just like

162
00:05:27,567 --> 00:05:29,420
a pack of Lucky Strikes.

163
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,130
We'd stand on the corner with one

164
00:05:32,130 --> 00:05:33,126
in our mouth,

165
00:05:33,126 --> 00:05:36,040
blowing pretend smoke in the cold air,

166
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:37,727
feeling very grown up.

167
00:05:37,907 --> 00:05:39,242
Wax lips.

168
00:05:39,422 --> 00:05:43,149
Wax bottles with colored sugar water inside.

169
00:05:43,329 --> 00:05:44,710
Bit-O-Honey.

170
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:46,713
Black Jack gum.

171
00:05:46,893 --> 00:05:48,240
Mary Janes.

172
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:50,250
And here's the thing.

173
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:51,785
When we came home,

174
00:05:51,785 --> 00:05:54,796
our pillowcases weighed as much as Danny

175
00:05:54,796 --> 00:05:55,097
did.

176
00:05:55,277 --> 00:05:57,235
We'd dump it all out on the

177
00:05:57,235 --> 00:05:59,120
living room rug and trade.

178
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:02,179
Three Mary Janes for one Hershey bar.

179
00:06:02,359 --> 00:06:04,440
My mother never looked at any of

180
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:04,635
it.

181
00:06:04,815 --> 00:06:06,815
We ate until we were sick,

182
00:06:06,815 --> 00:06:08,814
and then we ate some more,

183
00:06:08,814 --> 00:06:11,583
and nobody went to the dentist about

184
00:06:11,583 --> 00:06:12,814
it until Easter.

185
00:06:13,164 --> 00:06:14,440
On Halloween day,

186
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,391
school was half a holiday.

187
00:06:16,571 --> 00:06:18,652
We wore our costumes to class,

188
00:06:18,652 --> 00:06:20,871
and in the afternoon there was a

189
00:06:20,871 --> 00:06:22,883
parade around the playground,

190
00:06:22,883 --> 00:06:24,409
every grade in a line,

191
00:06:24,409 --> 00:06:27,252
while the mothers watched from the fence.

192
00:06:27,432 --> 00:06:29,293
My teacher, Miss Harmon,

193
00:06:29,293 --> 00:06:32,085
came dressed as a witch every single

194
00:06:32,085 --> 00:06:33,946
year, with a green face.

195
00:06:34,126 --> 00:06:36,473
She looked so real that the first

196
00:06:36,473 --> 00:06:37,469
graders cried.

197
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:40,571
And every town had a haunted house.

198
00:06:40,751 --> 00:06:43,399
Ours was the old Albright place on

199
00:06:43,399 --> 00:06:46,202
Second Street, a big gray house with

200
00:06:46,202 --> 00:06:47,526
a broken shutter,

201
00:06:47,526 --> 00:06:50,251
where an old widow lived alone with

202
00:06:50,251 --> 00:06:51,653
about eleven cats.

203
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:54,733
On Halloween the bravest boys would run

204
00:06:54,733 --> 00:06:57,038
up and touch her front door and

205
00:06:57,038 --> 00:06:57,708
run back.

206
00:06:57,888 --> 00:07:00,086
Years later I found out she sat

207
00:07:00,086 --> 00:07:02,496
in the dark every Halloween with a

208
00:07:02,496 --> 00:07:03,773
big bowl of candy,

209
00:07:03,773 --> 00:07:05,829
waiting for someone to knock.

210
00:07:06,009 --> 00:07:07,623
Nobody ever did.

211
00:07:07,803 --> 00:07:09,916
That still breaks my heart.

212
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:11,450
Number four.

213
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:13,278
And this is the one.

214
00:07:13,458 --> 00:07:14,688
In those days,

215
00:07:14,688 --> 00:07:18,642
sweetheart, trick or treat meant exactly what

216
00:07:18,642 --> 00:07:19,345
it said.

217
00:07:19,525 --> 00:07:21,566
If you didn't give a treat,

218
00:07:21,566 --> 00:07:22,776
you got a trick.

219
00:07:22,956 --> 00:07:25,045
And the big boys meant it.

220
00:07:25,225 --> 00:07:27,909
They carried bars of soap in their

221
00:07:27,909 --> 00:07:30,513
pockets, and if a house was dark,

222
00:07:30,513 --> 00:07:33,039
they soaped every single window.

223
00:07:33,219 --> 00:07:34,803
Big white swirls,

224
00:07:34,803 --> 00:07:36,574
all over the glass.

225
00:07:36,924 --> 00:07:38,352
They threw eggs.

226
00:07:38,532 --> 00:07:41,121
They let the air out of tires.

227
00:07:41,301 --> 00:07:43,681
They took the gate off Mr.

228
00:07:43,861 --> 00:07:45,901
Weber's fence and hung it in the

229
00:07:45,901 --> 00:07:47,367
tree in the schoolyard.

230
00:07:47,547 --> 00:07:49,755
They tied tin cans to the bumper

231
00:07:49,755 --> 00:07:51,274
of the minister's car.

232
00:07:51,454 --> 00:07:54,277
And the night before Halloween they called

233
00:07:54,277 --> 00:07:56,495
Mischief Night, or Cabbage Night,

234
00:07:56,495 --> 00:07:58,646
and in some towns the boys threw

235
00:07:58,646 --> 00:08:01,267
rotten cabbages from the farms at front

236
00:08:01,267 --> 00:08:01,671
doors.

237
00:08:02,021 --> 00:08:03,541
Out in the country,

238
00:08:03,541 --> 00:08:06,421
the farm boys tipped over outhouses.

239
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:09,164
That was the great Halloween prank of

240
00:08:09,164 --> 00:08:10,827
America for fifty years.

241
00:08:11,007 --> 00:08:13,481
Four boys, one outhouse,

242
00:08:13,481 --> 00:08:14,409
one push.

243
00:08:14,589 --> 00:08:16,641
The farmer would come out in the

244
00:08:16,641 --> 00:08:18,822
morning in his long johns and find

245
00:08:18,822 --> 00:08:20,554
it lying on its back in the

246
00:08:20,554 --> 00:08:20,939
field.

247
00:08:21,119 --> 00:08:23,276
My father said he tipped three of

248
00:08:23,276 --> 00:08:25,105
them in nineteen thirty-two,

249
00:08:25,105 --> 00:08:26,935
and he was proud of it until

250
00:08:26,935 --> 00:08:27,981
the day he died.

251
00:08:28,331 --> 00:08:30,800
Some towns had so much trouble that

252
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:34,187
the police and the businessmen started Halloween

253
00:08:34,187 --> 00:08:35,598
parades and parties,

254
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:37,503
just to keep the boys busy.

255
00:08:37,683 --> 00:08:40,006
Free cider, free doughnuts,

256
00:08:40,006 --> 00:08:43,104
a costume contest at the school gym.

257
00:08:43,284 --> 00:08:45,704
Anything to keep them away from the

258
00:08:45,704 --> 00:08:46,257
windows.

259
00:08:46,437 --> 00:08:49,958
Today, sweetheart, soaping a man's car would

260
00:08:49,958 --> 00:08:52,439
get you a ticket for vandalism.

261
00:08:52,619 --> 00:08:54,866
Back then you got a talking-to and

262
00:08:54,866 --> 00:08:56,055
a bucket of water.

263
00:08:56,406 --> 00:08:57,903
Number five.

264
00:08:58,083 --> 00:08:59,384
The parties.

265
00:08:59,564 --> 00:09:01,157
At home or at church,

266
00:09:01,157 --> 00:09:03,813
there was always a Halloween party.

267
00:09:03,993 --> 00:09:06,629
And the favorite game was bobbing for

268
00:09:06,629 --> 00:09:07,128
apples.

269
00:09:07,308 --> 00:09:09,889
A big washtub full of cold water

270
00:09:09,889 --> 00:09:12,068
and apples floating on top.

271
00:09:12,248 --> 00:09:15,104
Every child in the neighborhood put their

272
00:09:15,104 --> 00:09:17,054
whole face in that same tub,

273
00:09:17,054 --> 00:09:18,447
one after the other,

274
00:09:18,447 --> 00:09:20,886
with their hands behind their back,

275
00:09:20,886 --> 00:09:22,557
trying to bite an apple.

276
00:09:22,737 --> 00:09:24,165
The same water.

277
00:09:24,345 --> 00:09:25,599
For two hours.

278
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:29,015
Then the haunted house in somebody's basement.

279
00:09:29,195 --> 00:09:31,673
They'd turn the lights off and lead

280
00:09:31,673 --> 00:09:33,940
you down the stairs blindfolded,

281
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:35,993
and make you put your hand in

282
00:09:35,993 --> 00:09:37,764
a bowl of cold spaghetti,

283
00:09:37,764 --> 00:09:39,605
and tell you it was a dead

284
00:09:39,605 --> 00:09:40,526
man's brains.

285
00:09:40,706 --> 00:09:43,283
Peeled grapes were the eyeballs.

286
00:09:43,463 --> 00:09:46,361
I screamed so loud at the Kowalskis'

287
00:09:46,361 --> 00:09:48,293
basement party that Mrs.

288
00:09:48,473 --> 00:09:51,155
Kowalski dropped a tray of cider.

289
00:09:51,505 --> 00:09:54,422
And the big kids told ghost stories

290
00:09:54,422 --> 00:09:55,673
by candlelight.

291
00:09:55,853 --> 00:09:58,664
About the hitchhiker who disappeared from the

292
00:09:58,664 --> 00:09:59,289
back seat.

293
00:09:59,469 --> 00:10:01,698
About the hook on the car door.

294
00:10:01,878 --> 00:10:04,639
Every teenager in America knew those stories

295
00:10:04,639 --> 00:10:05,580
in the fifties,

296
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,462
and every one of them swore it

297
00:10:07,462 --> 00:10:09,030
happened to their cousin.

298
00:10:09,210 --> 00:10:11,477
I didn't sleep for a week after

299
00:10:11,477 --> 00:10:12,136
the hook.

300
00:10:12,316 --> 00:10:14,557
Danny slept just fine.

301
00:10:14,907 --> 00:10:17,893
Now, one good thing that started back

302
00:10:17,893 --> 00:10:18,297
then.

303
00:10:18,477 --> 00:10:20,067
Around nineteen fifty,

304
00:10:20,067 --> 00:10:23,899
some Sunday school children in Pennsylvania collected

305
00:10:23,899 --> 00:10:26,646
coins on Halloween for the children of

306
00:10:26,646 --> 00:10:28,670
the world, instead of candy.

307
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:31,586
That became Trick-or-Treat for UNICEF,

308
00:10:31,586 --> 00:10:33,529
with the little orange box.

309
00:10:33,709 --> 00:10:36,313
By the late fifties we carried those

310
00:10:36,313 --> 00:10:38,845
boxes too, and you'd hear the coins

311
00:10:38,845 --> 00:10:40,942
rattling all down Elm Street.

312
00:10:41,122 --> 00:10:43,195
Mother made us put in a nickel

313
00:10:43,195 --> 00:10:43,955
of our own.

314
00:10:44,305 --> 00:10:46,162
So when did it all change?

315
00:10:46,342 --> 00:10:47,794
Little by little.

316
00:10:47,974 --> 00:10:50,706
In the late sixties and seventies,

317
00:10:50,706 --> 00:10:54,804
the newspapers started printing scary stories about

318
00:10:54,804 --> 00:10:57,697
razor blades in apples and poison in

319
00:10:57,697 --> 00:10:58,179
candy.

320
00:10:58,359 --> 00:11:00,809
Most of those stories turned out not

321
00:11:00,809 --> 00:11:01,557
to be true,

322
00:11:01,557 --> 00:11:02,306
sweetheart.

323
00:11:02,486 --> 00:11:04,344
But parents got frightened.

324
00:11:04,524 --> 00:11:07,891
Hospitals started offering to X-ray your candy.

325
00:11:08,071 --> 00:11:12,099
And the homemade popcorn balls disappeared forever.

326
00:11:12,449 --> 00:11:14,405
Then the costumes changed,

327
00:11:14,405 --> 00:11:16,964
and the masks got bigger eyeholes,

328
00:11:16,964 --> 00:11:20,276
and the parents came along with flashlights,

329
00:11:20,276 --> 00:11:22,835
and the candy came in tiny wrapped

330
00:11:22,835 --> 00:11:23,211
bars.

331
00:11:23,391 --> 00:11:24,753
And it's safer,

332
00:11:24,753 --> 00:11:26,386
I know it's safer.

333
00:11:26,566 --> 00:11:29,504
I'm glad you have that light-up bucket.

334
00:11:29,684 --> 00:11:31,623
But I'll tell you a secret.

335
00:11:31,803 --> 00:11:33,951
I miss the popcorn balls.

336
00:11:34,131 --> 00:11:36,011
I miss that hot penny.

337
00:11:36,191 --> 00:11:38,136
I even miss the soap on the

338
00:11:38,136 --> 00:11:39,361
windows a little.

339
00:11:39,711 --> 00:11:42,202
So here are my three Halloween rules

340
00:11:42,202 --> 00:11:43,933
from nineteen fifty-four,

341
00:11:43,933 --> 00:11:45,109
for you, tonight.

342
00:11:45,289 --> 00:11:47,879
One, take a little brother or sister

343
00:11:47,879 --> 00:11:49,318
and hold their hand.

344
00:11:49,498 --> 00:11:52,158
Two, say thank you at every single

345
00:11:52,158 --> 00:11:54,662
door, even at the house with the

346
00:11:54,662 --> 00:11:55,210
apples.

347
00:11:55,390 --> 00:11:58,294
And three, when you get home,

348
00:11:58,294 --> 00:11:59,396
trade fair.

349
00:11:59,576 --> 00:12:02,722
Three Mary Janes for one chocolate bar.

350
00:12:02,902 --> 00:12:04,249
That's the rate.

351
00:12:04,429 --> 00:12:07,087
It's been the rate for seventy years.

352
00:12:07,438 --> 00:12:08,761
And you know what?

353
00:12:08,941 --> 00:12:11,214
If you want a popcorn ball like

354
00:12:11,214 --> 00:12:11,507
Mrs.

355
00:12:11,687 --> 00:12:14,729
Dietrich's, I'll make you one tomorrow.

356
00:12:14,909 --> 00:12:17,986
Popcorn, molasses, butter and a pinch of

357
00:12:17,986 --> 00:12:20,525
salt, and you shape them with wet

358
00:12:20,525 --> 00:12:22,525
hands so they don't stick.

359
00:12:22,705 --> 00:12:26,397
Your great-grandmother Dorothy made them every year.

360
00:12:26,577 --> 00:12:28,365
We'll make them together.

361
00:12:28,715 --> 00:12:30,944
Now tell me in the comments.

362
00:12:31,124 --> 00:12:33,123
What was the best thing you ever

363
00:12:33,123 --> 00:12:35,060
got in your trick-or-treat bag?

364
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:38,193
And did anybody in your town really

365
00:12:38,193 --> 00:12:39,965
tip over an outhouse?

366
00:12:40,145 --> 00:12:41,666
I want names.

367
00:12:41,846 --> 00:12:44,412
Happy Halloween, sweetheart.

368
00:12:44,592 --> 00:12:47,177
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

369
00:12:47,177 --> 00:12:48,829
another story tomorrow.
